  WHY L = -INFINITY AT AN F OPENING, DERIVED AND THEN MEASURED

  THE CLAIM: at an opening, an admissible rival lies below the entrant in p
  if and only if its node count rho = n - l - 1 is strictly smaller.
  Checked at all 24 openings of the observed order: HOLDS at every one

  EVERY OPENING, AND WHETHER ANYTHING LIES BELOW IT
       Z el  sub   rho  admissible rivals of smaller node count            L
       1 H   1s      0  (none)                                          -inf
       3 Li  2s      1  2p, 3d, 4f                                    finite
       5 B   2p      0  (none)                                          -inf
      11 Na  3s      2  3p, 3d, 4d, 4f, 5f                            finite
      13 Al  3p      1  3d, 4f                                        finite
      19 K   4s      3  3d, 4p, 4d, 4f, 5d, 5f, 6f                    finite
      21 Sc  3d      0  (none)                                          -inf
      31 Ga  4p      2  4d, 4f, 5f                                    finite
      37 Rb  5s      4  4d, 4f, 5p, 5d, 5f, 6d, 6f, 7f                finite
      39 Y   4d      1  4f                                            finite
      47 Ag  5s      4  4f, 5p, 5d, 5f, 6d, 6f, 7f                    finite
      49 In  5p      3  4f, 5d, 5f, 6f                                finite
      55 Cs  6s      5  4f, 5d, 5f, 6p, 6d, 6f, 7d, 7f                finite
      57 La  5d      2  4f, 5f                                        finite
      58 Ce  4f      0  (none)                                          -inf
      64 Gd  5d      2  4f, 5f                                        finite
      71 Lu  5d      2  5f                                            finite
      81 Tl  6p      4  5f, 6d, 6f, 7f                                finite
      87 Fr  7s      6  5f, 6d, 6f, 7p, 7d, 7f                        finite
      89 Ac  6d      3  5f, 6f                                        finite
      91 Pa  5f      1  (none)                                          -inf
      96 Cm  6d      3  5f, 6f                                        finite
     103 Lr  7p      5  6d, 6f, 7d, 7f                                finite
     104 Rf  6d      3  6f                                            finite

  L = -INFINITY AT 5 OF 24 OPENINGS: 1s (Z 1, H), 2p (Z 5, B), 3d (Z 21, Sc), 4f (Z 58, Ce), 5f (Z 91, Pa)

  AND THE TWO REASONS ARE DIFFERENT
    1s  rho = 0.  Nothing has fewer nodes than none -- the node floor,
        which is the reason section 34.9 gives, and at this opening it is right.
    2p  rho = 0.  Nothing has fewer nodes than none -- the node floor,
        which is the reason section 34.9 gives, and at this opening it is right.
    3d  rho = 0.  Nothing has fewer nodes than none -- the node floor,
        which is the reason section 34.9 gives, and at this opening it is right.
    4f  rho = 0.  Nothing has fewer nodes than none -- the node floor,
        which is the reason section 34.9 gives, and at this opening it is right.
    5f  rho = 1, so the floor is NOT the reason.  Every subshell of
        smaller node count -- 1s, 2p, 3d, 4f -- is FULL at Th (Z 90) and so fails the law's
        own admissibility test q < 2(2l+1).  The floor is reached by exhaustion.

  WHICH l HAS EVERY OPENING OUTSIDE THE DOMAIN
    s: 1 of 8 openings have L = -inf   (1s)
    p: 1 of 6 openings have L = -inf   (2p)
    d: 1 of 8 openings have L = -inf   (3d)
    f: 2 of 2 openings have L = -inf   (4f, 5f)  <== EVERY ONE

  So section 34.9's conclusion is true and its reason is not.  The true reason
  covers both f openings in one clause: AT EVERY F OPENING NO ADMISSIBLE SUBSHELL
  HAS A SMALLER NODE COUNT -- at 4f because none exists, at 5f because every one
  of them is full.  And f is the ONLY l of which that is true at every opening,
  which is what 'f is outside the domain' should mean.  Nothing is repaired here.
